   "gap": "Partial against the success condition 'Each estimate and theorem has explicit model, sampling and regularity scope' for the selected extent (Fisher 1922's complete estimation, information and sufficiency presentation, retaining all stated and necessary regularity qualifications). These selected source arguments are not reconstructed as statements with explicit model, sampling and regularity scope and written proofs: (1) Fisher p. 322: the standard error pi/(2 sqrt n) of the median of a large Cauchy sample (asymptotic normality of the sample median with variance pi^2/(4n)); fisher-estimation-and-regularity.tex 304-321 proves only consistency and says it 'does not claim the paper's more precise asymptotic median error'. (2) Fisher p. 317: the fourfold-table (double dichotomy of a normal surface) example, in which the criterion of consistency determines the correlation uniquely from the four sample fractions; absent from the route. (3) Fisher pp. 346-347, figs. 3-4: the location and scale efficiency contours on the Type I/II side of the normal point (y>0) are not shown to be the efficiencies of those curves (owned body 1130-1133: they 'rest on Fisher's calculation alone'). (4) Fisher p. 355: that moments have at least 80 per cent efficiency for the form of symmetric Pearson curves exactly when beta_2 lies between 2.65 and 3.42 needs E_r increasing in r on each side, which is 'not proved here' (owned body 1438-1442). (5) Fisher pp. 354-355, Type II form parameters: condition (C) is not verified, so the inverse-information values are not shown to be limit variances of a root of the likelihood equations (owned body 1443-1447). (6) Fisher p. 337: Type III location at p=1 determined faster than sqrt n 'whether or not p and a are known'; the unknown-scale (and unknown-shape) case 'is not proved here' (owned body 1607-1611), thm:p15-w02b-p15-057-linear-end assuming a and the shape known.",
